.SH NAME
ktm-guide-threads-and-threadlocal \- Threads and threadlocal (chapter 27 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
A ktm program can run several threads at once, each with its own stack, all sharing the program's globals and heap. The thread (see ktm-thread(3)) module starts them and gives them locks to take turns with; the language adds \fBthreadlocal\fR, a global with one copy per thread. This chapter covers starting threads, errors in them, sharing data safely, \fBthreadlocal\fR, many threads at once (WaitGroup, each, channels, pools), coroutines in brief, and C's threads.
.SH Starting a thread
.PP
\fBthread.spawn(&f, &arg)\fR runs \fBf(&arg)\fR on a new thread and returns a \fBthread.Thread\fR; its \fBjoin()\fR waits for it to end. The function takes a pointer to any type, so a thread gets its input and leaves its result in the same struct:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import thread;

struct Part {
    i64 from, to;
    i64 sum;
};

void add(Part *p) {
    for (i64 i = p.from; i < p.to; i++) {
        p.sum += i;
    }
}

void main() {
    Part parts[4];
    thread.Thread t[4];
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        parts[i].from = i * 250 + 1;
        parts[i].to = (i + 1) * 250 + 1;
        t[i] = thread.spawn(&add, &parts[i]);
    }
    i64 total = 0;
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        t[i].join();
        total += parts[i].sum;
    }
    printf("1 + 2 + ... + 1000 = %d\en", total);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1 + 2 + ... + 1000 = 500500
.EE
.RE
.PP
Each thread wrote only its own \fBPart\fR, so they didn't need to take turns. The argument is shared with the caller: keep it alive until \fBjoin()\fR returns.
.PP
A function with no argument is started with \fBthread.spawn(&f)\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import thread;

i64 gDone;

void work() {
    gDone = 1;
}

void main() {
    thread.Thread t = thread.spawn(&work);
    t.join();
    printf("done: %d\en", gDone);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
done: 1
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
A \fBThread\fR is joined once; joining it again, or joining a \fBThread\fR never started, does nothing.
.IP \(bu 2
A thread that is never joined keeps a little memory until the program ends.
.IP \(bu 2
A spawned thread has all of ktm's runtime: a crash in it is reported with its frames, \fBtry\fR works, and \fBthreadlocal\fR globals are its own.
.SH Many threads at once
.SS go and WaitGroup
.PP
To start many threads and wait for all of them, \fBthread.go(&f, &arg, &wg)\fR starts one and counts it in a \fBthread.WaitGroup\fR; \fBwg.wait()\fR returns when every one has finished. There is nothing to join:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import thread;

struct Job {
    i64 n;
    i64 square;
};

void work(Job *j) {
    j.square = j.n * j.n;
}

void main() {
    Job jobs[5];
    thread.WaitGroup wg;
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        jobs[i].n = i + 1;
        thread.go(&work, &jobs[i], &wg);
    }
    wg.wait();
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        printf("%d ", jobs[i].square);
    }
    printf("\en");
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1 4 9 16 25 
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBwg.wait()\fR throws the first error one of its threads threw. A \fBWaitGroup\fR can count work of your own too: \fBwg.add(n)\fR before it starts, \fBwg.done()\fR as each piece ends.
.SS each
.PP
\fBthread.each(items, &f)\fR runs \fBf\fR on every element of a slice, split over as many threads as the machine has cores, and returns when all are done. It is the simplest way to use every core:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import thread;

struct Image {
    i64 id;
    i64 pixels;
};

void shrink(Image *img) {
    img.pixels = img.pixels / 4;
}

void main() {
    Image images[6];
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
        images[i].id = i;
        images[i].pixels = 1000 * (i + 1);
    }
    thread.each(images[0:6], &shrink);
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
        printf("%d ", images[i].pixels);
    }
    printf("\en");
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
250 500 750 1000 1250 1500 
.EE
.RE
.PP
Like \fBwg.wait()\fR, \fBeach\fR throws the first error one of the calls threw.
.SS Channels
.PP
A \fBthread.Channel<T>\fR passes values from thread to thread: \fBsend(v)\fR puts one in, \fBrecv(&v)\fR takes the oldest out. \fBthread.channel<T>(n)\fR makes one that holds up to \fBn\fR values: a send waits while it is full, a receive while it is empty. \fBclose()\fR says no more will come: \fBrecv\fR returns false once a closed channel is empty. \fBscoped\fR frees it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import thread;

void produce(thread.Channel<i64> *ch) {
    for (i64 i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
        ch.send(i * 10);
    }
    ch.close();
}

void main() {
    scoped thread.Channel<i64> ch = thread.channel<i64>(2);
    thread.Thread t = thread.spawn(&produce, &ch);
    i64 v;
    i64 total = 0;
    while (ch.recv(&v)) {
        total += v;
    }
    t.join();
    printf("received %d in all\en", total);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
received 150 in all
.EE
.RE
.PP
A channel made with no size (\fBthread.channel<T>()\fR) holds nothing: each send waits until a receive takes the value. Sending on a closed channel throws.
.SS A pool of threads
.PP
Starting a thread costs some microseconds. For many small jobs, a \fBthread.Pool\fR keeps a few threads running and hands them the jobs: \fBthread.pool(n)\fR starts \fBn\fR (0 for one per core), \fBp.run(&f, &arg)\fR queues a job, \fBp.wait()\fR waits until the queue is done and throws the first error a job threw. \fBscoped\fR stops it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import thread;

struct Job {
    i64 n;
    i64 result;
};

void triple(Job *j) {
    j.result = j.n * 3;
}

void main() {
    Job jobs[8];
    scoped thread.Pool *p = thread.pool(2);
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
        jobs[i].n = i;
        p.run(&triple, &jobs[i]);
    }
    p.wait();
    printf("%d %d\en", jobs[1].result, jobs[7].result);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
3 21
.EE
.RE
.SH Errors in threads
.PP
What a thread throws comes back from \fBjoin()\fR: the same code, message, file and line, thrown in the thread that joins. A \fBtry\fR around the \fBjoin()\fR catches it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import thread;

enum Err { badInput };

struct Job {
    i64 n;
};

void check(Job *j) {
    if (j.n < 0) {
        throw(Err.badInput, "job %d: negative input", j.n);
    }
}

void main() {
    Job j = {\-3};
    thread.Thread t = thread.spawn(&check, &j);
    try {
        t.join();
    } catch (Error e) {
        printf("the thread failed: %s\en", e.message);
    }
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
the thread failed: job \-3: negative input
.EE
.RE
.PP
Within a thread, \fBtry\fR and \fBcatch\fR work as anywhere else: each thread has its own chain of \fBtry\fR blocks, and a throw is caught only by a \fBtry\fR of its own thread before \fBjoin()\fR sees it.
.SH Sharing data
.PP
When threads change the same data, they must take turns. A \fBthread.Mutex\fR is held by one thread at a time: \fBlock()\fR waits until no other thread holds it. It needs no setup and nothing to free:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import thread;

thread.Mutex gLock;
i64 gTotal = 0;

void add() {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {
        gLock.lock();
        gTotal++;
        gLock.unlock();
    }
}

void main() {
    thread.Thread t[4];
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        t[i] = thread.spawn(&add);
    }
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        t[i].join();
    }
    printf("%d\en", gTotal);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
40000
.EE
.RE
.PP
Without the lock some of the 40000 additions would be lost: two threads read the same value and both write it plus one.
.PP
\fBguard()\fR locks and returns a \fBthread.Guard\fR that unlocks when it is freed, so with \fBscoped\fR the lock is held until the end of the block, however the block is left (a \fBreturn\fR, a \fBbreak\fR, a throw):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import thread;

thread.Mutex gLock;
i64 gBalance = 100;

bool withdraw(i64 amount) {
    scoped thread.Guard g = gLock.guard();
    if (gBalance < amount) {
        return false;
    }
    gBalance \-= amount;
    return true;
}

void main() {
    printf("%d %d %d\en", withdraw(60), withdraw(60), gBalance);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1 0 40
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBtryLock()\fR takes the lock only if it is free, and says whether it did.
.SS Waiting for a condition
.PP
A \fBthread.Cond\fR lets a thread sleep until another one says something changed. The waiting thread holds the mutex, checks the condition in a loop, and \fBwait(&m)\fR lets go of the mutex while it sleeps:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import thread;

thread.Mutex gM;
thread.Cond gReady;
i64 gQueued;
i64 gTaken;

void consume() {
    for (i64 n = 0; n < 3; n++) {
        gM.lock();
        while (gQueued == 0) {
            gReady.wait(&gM);
        }
        gQueued\-\-;
        gTaken++;
        gM.unlock();
    }
}

void main() {
    thread.Thread t = thread.spawn(&consume);
    for (i64 n = 0; n < 3; n++) {
        gM.lock();
        gQueued++;
        gReady.signal();
        gM.unlock();
    }
    t.join();
    printf("taken %d\en", gTaken);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
taken 3
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBsignal()\fR wakes one waiting thread, \fBbroadcast()\fR all of them.
.SS Readers and writers, and running once
.PP
A \fBthread.RwLock\fR lets many threads read at once while no one writes: \fBreadLock()\fR and \fBreadUnlock()\fR around reading, \fBwriteLock()\fR and \fBwriteUnlock()\fR around changing. A \fBthread.Once\fR runs a function once, however many threads ask:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import thread;

thread.Once gSetup;
i64 gSetups;

void setup() {
    gSetups++;
}

void worker() {
    gSetup.run(&setup);
}

void main() {
    thread.Thread t[4];
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        t[i] = thread.spawn(&worker);
    }
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        t[i].join();
    }
    printf("setup ran %d time\en", gSetups);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
setup ran 1 time
.EE
.RE
.SS Atomics
.PP
For a single integer, the atomic (see ktm-atomic(3)) module is simpler and faster than a lock:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import atomic, thread;

i64 gHits = 0;

void worker() {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
        atomic.add(&gHits, 1);
    }
}

void main() {
    thread.Thread t[4];
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        t[i] = thread.spawn(&worker);
    }
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        t[i].join();
    }
    printf("%d hits\en", atomic.load(&gHits));
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
400000 hits
.EE
.RE
.SH threadlocal
.PP
A global marked \fBthreadlocal\fR has one copy per thread. Every thread starts with the declared value, and what one thread writes the others don't see:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import thread;

threadlocal i64 hits = 10;
i64 seen[2];

void work(i64 *k) {
    for (i64 i = 0; i <= *k; i++) {
        hits++;
    }
    seen[*k] = hits;
}

void main() {
    hits = 100;
    i64 ks[2] = {0, 1};
    thread.Thread t[2];
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
        t[i] = thread.spawn(&work, &ks[i]);
    }
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
        t[i].join();
    }
    printf("%d %d, main %d\en", seen[0], seen[1], hits);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
11 12, main 100
.EE
.RE
.PP
Main's \fBhits = 100\fR changed only main's copy; each thread started from 10.
.PP
A threadlocal is useful for per-thread state that would otherwise be passed through every call: a buffer each thread reuses, a counter, the current request:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import thread;

threadlocal u8 *gWorker = "main";

void say(u8 *what) {
    printf("[%s] %s\en", gWorker, what);
}

void run(u8 *name) {
    gWorker = name;
    say("started");
}

void main() {
    thread.Thread t = thread.spawn(&run, "worker\-1");
    t.join();
    say("still main");
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
[worker\-1] started
[main] still main
.EE
.RE
.PP
A struct or an array can be threadlocal too, each thread getting its own:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import thread;

struct Stats {
    i64 calls;
    i64 errors;
};

threadlocal Stats gStats;

void busy() {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        gStats.calls++;
    }
    gStats.errors = 1;
    printf("thread: %d calls, %d errors\en", gStats.calls, gStats.errors);
}

void main() {
    gStats.calls = 1;
    thread.Thread t = thread.spawn(&busy);
    t.join();
    printf("main: %d calls, %d errors\en", gStats.calls, gStats.errors);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
thread: 5 calls, 1 errors
main: 1 calls, 0 errors
.EE
.RE
.PP
The thread started from a zeroed \fBStats\fR of its own; main's \fBcalls = 1\fR and the thread's changes never met.
.SS threadlocal rules
.IP \(bu 2
Only a global can be \fBthreadlocal\fR: \fBthreadlocal i64 x;\fR, \fBstatic threadlocal ...\fR, \fBpub threadlocal ...\fR, \fBextern threadlocal ...\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
The initial value must be a constant: a number, a string, \fB{...}\fR, or the address of an ordinary global.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB&x\fR is the calling thread's copy. A coroutine resumed on another thread sees that thread's copies.
.IP \(bu 2
Compiled programs reach a threadlocal in one or two instructions through the thread pointer (on macOS through the system's thread-local call). The JIT keeps a copy per thread, a little slower, and can't reach the threadlocal of a module built with \fB\-lib\fR: read it through a function of the module there.
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
i32 main() {
    threadlocal i64 n = 0;
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
`threadlocal` only goes on a global variable, not a local
.EE
.RE
.SH Coroutines in brief
.PP
A coroutine is a function that can pause in the middle and be resumed later, on its own small stack, in the same thread. Thousands of them are cheap. The \fBcoro\fR module provides them: as generators, and as the event loop behind ktm's network servers, where a coroutine waiting for the network lets the others run.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import coro;

void *count(void *arg) {
    for (i64 i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
        coro.yield((void *)i);
    }
    return null;
}

i32 main() {
    coro.Coroutine *c = coro.spawn(&count);
    while (true) {
        i64 v = (i64)coro.next(c);
        if (coro.done(c)) {
            break;
        }
        printf("%d ", v);
    }
    printf("\en");
    coro.release(c);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1 2 3 
.EE
.RE
.PP
See coro (see ktm-coro(3)) for generators, the event loop (\fBcoro.go\fR, \fBcoro.run\fR, \fBcoro.read\fR, \fBcoro.write\fR) and \fBcoro.serve\fR, which runs a loop on every core.
.SH C threads
.PP
The \fBthread\fR module is built on POSIX threads, which a program can also use directly through \fBlibc\fR, as C does: \fBlibc.pthread_create\fR starts a thread running a function of type \fBfn(void *) void *\fR, and \fBlibc.pthread_join\fR waits for it. Pass \fB&th\fR to create and \fBth\fR to join:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import libc;

i64 gSum;

void *add(void *arg) {
    gSum = (i64)arg * 2;
    return null;
}

void main() {
    libc.pthread_t *th;
    libc.pthread_create(&th, null, &add, (void *)21);
    libc.pthread_join(th);
    printf("%d\en", gSum);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
42
.EE
.RE
.PP
A thread started this way gets no help: what it throws ends the program unless it catches it, and its argument and result go through \fBvoid *\fR\&.
.SH Rules
.IP \(bu 2
\fBthread.spawn(&f, &arg)\fR runs \fBf(&arg)\fR on a new thread (\fBf\fR takes a pointer), and \fBthread.spawn(&f)\fR a function with no argument; \fBjoin()\fR waits for it.
.IP \(bu 2
What a thread throws, \fBjoin()\fR throws in the thread that joins; \fBWaitGroup.wait()\fR, \fBthread.each\fR and \fBPool.wait()\fR throw the first error their threads threw.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBthread.go\fR with a \fBWaitGroup\fR starts threads to wait for together, \fBthread.each\fR runs a function over a slice on every core, a \fBChannel<T>\fR passes values between threads, and a \fBPool\fR runs many small jobs on a few threads.
.IP \(bu 2
Threads share globals and the heap; protect shared changes with a \fBthread.Mutex\fR, or use \fBatomic\fR for a single integer. The module's locks need no setup and nothing to free; don't copy one that is in use.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBthreadlocal\fR on a global gives each thread its own copy, starting at the declared constant value.
.IP \(bu 2
Coroutines run inside a thread; see the \fBcoro\fR module.
.IP \(bu 2
C's threads are there too, through \fBlibc.pthread_*\fR\&.
